What this solution actually does: it is the last hop of the SWIFT *outgoing* chain. Every timer tick (interval from the external service configuration, 10 s fallback) it re-reads 14 parameters from TOMI (operating window, the SWF_OUT_GET_MT_BATCH flag, the alert address and ten MQ.SWF_OUT.* connection settings), reads every SWIFT message that an upstream service left in status INI (usp_ObtenerOperacionesSWFOutMq), and, when sending is allowed (always in "batch" mode, otherwise only on a business day inside HORA_OP_INICIO-HORA_OP_FIN), opens one TLS connection to IBM MQ and publishes each message text to the configured queue. For each message it first sets the TOMI control row to SNDNG, then performs the MQ put, then calls a procedure that sets PROC on three tables; a message-level MQ error sets ERROR. Failures are logged; unexpected exceptions raise an e-mail or a critical alert row; the tick always finishes by marking the service terminated. It carries outgoing payment-related SWIFT messages, so the findings below concern *delivered, delayed, lost or silently stuck* messages rather than style.
