5. Architecture-Fit Note
Apply the standing architecture question in _ORCHESTRATION.md (./_ORCHESTRATION.md). This workload is a continuous poll-and-publish relay (database rows -> MQ queue) with a small scheduling gate, not a multi-step saga and not a heavy batch job. A Windows Service is a reasonable host for a low-volume relay, and none of the findings is *caused* by the hosting model: they stem from a status protocol with no recovery (SOM-I3), failure classification (SOM-I4), silent failure modes (SOM-I1, I2, I5) and missing tests (SOM-T1). What the hosting model does expose is the "two systems, no shared transaction" problem: the DB status write and the MQ put cannot be atomic. Realistic options:
Option	Fits when	Trade-offs / unknowns
Keep the service; fix the status protocol (claim + SNDNG age alert + retry of the idempotent PROC write)	Volume is modest and an at-most-once relay with reconciliation is acceptable	Lowest disruption; needs the SQL changes in SOM-I3/S1 and an operations runbook
Keep the service, add an outbox/syncpoint discipline (MQ put and DB write coordinated, consumer de-duplicates on the folio)	Business requires effectively exactly-once delivery	More moving parts; requires an agreed MQMD contract with the SWIFT-gateway consumer (SOM-I7)
Modern .NET Worker	Shared Framework-4.8 library (MUFG_T24) and the IBM client are available for the new runtime	Blocked while MUFG_T24 is Framework-only; benefit is hosting APIs, not correctness
Scheduled job / SCM recovery	Messages are only needed at fixed times	Loses near-real-time hand-off; the window gate already behaves like a schedule
Event/message-driven (database change feed or broker-native trigger)	A change-notification mechanism exists for the control table	Removes polling but adds infrastructure; the unbounded read (SOM-S1) disappears
Serverless / microservice / full rewrite	A separate ownership, scale or isolation need exists	Highest cost; not justified by this code-quality review

Sequence: fix definite defects (SOM-I1, B1, I2-I5, T1) -> make the build reproducible and add the first real tests -> remove duplication with the GCMS twin (SOM-D1, GEN-4) -> then evaluate hosting. Unknown and needed from the owners: SLAs and daily volume, the consumer of the MQ queue (persistence, ids, ordering, de-duplication), the real queue/channel definitions and certificate renewal process, the deployed SQL objects, the holiday/weekend policy and who receives MAIL_TO/MAIL_ERROR.
